item.content:encoded:
Take a look at the following function:func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    defer resp.Body.Close()
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}
It sends a  request to  and returns the HTTP status code.Using HTTP/1 (), calling this function 100 times against
index.golang.org takes ~710 milliseconds on Go 1.26
on my machine. On Go 1.27, the same program takes ~150 milliseconds.That is almost 5 times faster! How is such a drastic performance difference
possible?The cost of establishing HTTP connectionsTo understand the difference, consider what happens on the network when we make
an HTTP request.Go’s  package supports HTTP/1.0, HTTP/1.1, and
HTTP/2. All three run over TCP, and usually over TLS when talking to HTTPS
endpoints. Making a request over a new connection looks something like this:: the client sends a  packet, the server
replies with , and the client replies with . Because the
client can send the first TLS message alongside that final ,
establishing the TCP connection takes 1 round-trip time (RTT).: next, a TLS handshake exchanges encryption keys
and verifies the server’s identity; using TLS 1.2, this takes 2 RTT.: with the keys exchanged, HTTP requests can finally be sent
after a total of 3 RTT.These round trips (plus some CPU time spent on TLS handshakes) add up quickly.
In cross-region communications, one-way latencies often reach or exceed the 50
ms (100 ms RTT) shown in the diagram above. Compared to the 300 ms spent just
setting up the connection, the HTTP request itself can be cheap to serve, taking
only a little over 1 RTT when bandwidth is plentiful or the HTTP response is
small.Even in more favorable conditions, opening a new connection can still be
expensive. Our opening benchmark queries
index.golang.org, which is distributed worldwide and
supports TLS 1.3. This means that latency is relatively low, and the TLS
handshake can actually finish in just 1 RTT. Even so, the performance difference
is still very noticeable.To avoid paying this setup cost on every request, HTTP clients and servers try
to reuse existing connections across multiple requests when possible. The
mechanics of this differ based on the HTTP version:: while not part of the official specification, clients and
servers often opt into connection reuse by sending a  header. Reuse continues until either endpoint omits the header
or sends a  header.: connections are persistent by default, and can be reused for
subsequent requests and responses unless either side declares otherwise by
sending a  header.: multiple requests and responses can be multiplexed over a single
TCP connection.When connection reuse failsIn HTTP/1 (both HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1), multiple requests and responses on the
same connection are sent sequentially on the wire. If two  requests are
sent to index.golang.org over the same TCP
connection, the responses arrive as a single stream of bytes:As shown in the diagram,  reads and parses
the status line and headers from the byte stream to populate
, then returns immediately. The
response body remains unread on the connection and is consumed only as the
caller reads from .Because responses are sent back-to-back on the same stream,
 can only find the start of the next response after
the current response body has been read to the very end (as determined by the
 or  header).Now look back at our example program, where we never read :func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    defer resp.Body.Close()
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}
Through Go 1.26, when  is called, 
sees that there is still an unread response body. Realizing that it cannot reuse
the connection, it abandons the existing connection and creates new connections
for its next requests, causing it to repeatedly incur the cost of establishing
new connections.(HTTP/2 does not have this problem. Because it multiplexes streams of frames
over a single connection rather than sending responses strictly back-to-back, an
unread response body on one stream does not block other requests from sharing
the connection.)Where failed connection reuse hurtsThis subtlety of HTTP/1 connection reuse can cause silent performance
degradation in seemingly innocuous code.As in our opening example, a client might only care about an API response’s
status code. However, the API endpoint it is talking to might send bodies that
provide more detailed but ultimately unused information, such as {"success": true, "message": "update processed"}. This will unfortunately cause connection
reuse to fail.Even programs that do read the response body can run into this issue if they
stop just short of the end of the body. For example,
 stops reading as soon as
it sees the closing  of a JSON object. If a server sends a JSON response
terminated with a newline, decoding the JSON object will leave the newline still
unread. The connection cannot be reused for a new request until that newline is
consumed. Thus, a healthy connection might end up being abandoned, even though
it was only a single byte away from being reusable.The unread body problem has been a long-standing sharp edge. Over the years,
many Go developers came up with their own workarounds. Most commonly, this is
done by draining the response body prior to closing it:func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    drainAndCloseBody(resp.Body)
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}

func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    io.Copy(io.Discard, body) // Drain the response body before closing it.
    body.Close()
}
While this works for small responses, if the unread body is large, establishing
a new TCP connection can be faster than downloading and discarding the rest of
the body. A better approach is to cap the number of bytes we are willing to
drain:func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    io.CopyN(io.Discard, body, 256<<10) // Only drain up to 256 KB.
    body.Close()
}
However, a server might send a response body that trickles in slowly, so we also
need to cap the time we spend draining:func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    drainDone := make(chan struct{}, 1)
    go func() {
        io.CopyN(io.Discard, body, 256<<10) // Only drain up to 256 KB.
        drainDone <- struct{}{}
    }()
    // Only spend at most 50 ms trying to drain the response body.
    select {
    case <-drainDone:
    case <-time.After(50 * time.Millisecond):
        // If we reach the 50 ms timeout, body.Close will run and interrupt the
        // io.CopyN, preventing goroutine leaks.
    }
    body.Close()
}
Of course, we also do not want to block for up to 50 milliseconds when closing a
response body. Instead, we can drain the body asynchronously in the background
and let it race against subsequent requests:func drainAndCloseBody(body io.ReadCloser) {
    drainDone := make(chan struct{}, 1)
    go func() {
        io.CopyN(io.Discard, body, 256<<10) // Only drain up to 256 KB.
        drainDone <- struct{}{}
    }()
    go func() {
        // Only spend at most 50 ms trying to drain the response body.
        select {
        case <-drainDone:
            // If the drain can finish before a new request is sent, the
            // connection will be reused; otherwise, a new connection is
            // created, and the current connection will be put in a pool of
            // idle connections, potentially being reused for another future
            // request.
        case <-time.After(50 * time.Millisecond):
            // If we reach the 50 ms timeout, body.Close will run and interrupt
            // the io.CopyN, preventing goroutine leaks.
        }
        body.Close()
    }()
}
As we can see, draining response bodies safely is surprisingly tricky and
cumbersome. Worse, as the practice of draining response bodies spread, some
nuances were often lost. This has led to misunderstandings such as attempting to
drain response bodies for HTTP/2 connections, when doing so is unnecessary.The current state and conclusionStarting in Go 1.27, 
automatically does everything we have implemented in the code above: it will
asynchronously drain an unread HTTP/1 response body for up to 50 ms or 256 KB.
It also implements additional optimizations; for example, when a response
contains the  header, draining the body is guaranteed to be
wasted work, and it therefore skips doing so. Note that this behavior might
evolve in future versions of Go, as other possible enhancements become apparent.As a general best practice, we now recommend that users simply close their HTTP
bodies once they no longer need them, like in our original example:func getStatus(url string) (int, error) {
    resp, err := http.Get(url)
    if err != nil {
        return 0, err
    }
    defer resp.Body.Close()
    return resp.StatusCode, nil
}
Go is all about simplicity. Let your code stay clean and simple, and let
 deal with the complexities on your behalf.

Existing benchmarks measure solve rate and cost, but don't punish excess code, which makes a codebase harder to work with in the long term. Parsimony measures how much code AI models add.It takes the patches that benchmarks already publish and counts : syntax elements like statements, calls, comparisons, names and literals. Lines aren't counted, so cramming code isn't rewarded, and deleted code is subtracted: net units = added − removed.Each patch is applied to the task's code, and both versions of every changed file are parsed. Nothing is run.Tests, docs and generated files are left out. Only Python is measured.Pass/fail comes from SWE-bench's published results; tests aren't rerun.Smaller isn't always better: units measure size, not readability or design.Models that write more code also tend to solve more tasks (Spearman 0.33–0.60 on the boards with 26+ models).Every model uses the same agent (mini-SWE-agent), but the agent still affects how much code gets written.Have code-size numbers from another benchmark? Download the example file, fill in your results and open it to get the same chart and table. The file stays in your browser. Format and how to share it.Example: check for a negative numberBoth return the same result.def has_negative(values):
    found = False
    for value in values:
        if value < 0:
            found = True
    return founddef has_negative(values):
    return any(value < 0 for value in values)

Today, we’re announcing AWS Lambda Web Functions, a new form factor that lets you run standard HTTP web applications on Lambda without rewriting your code into an event handler. You can take a standard port-listening server built with Express, Hono, Fastify, Next.js, or any Node.js framework, deploy it with a single command, and Lambda automatically handles scaling, multi-region distribution, and active CPU billing. No adapters, no infrastructure to manage, and no framework lock-in.With this launch, AWS Lambda now offers three distinct form factors, each built on the same Firecracker isolation technology and designed for a different programming model. are what you already know as Lambda functions. They use the event-driven, single-concurrent handler model with wall-clock billing. We’re introducing the “Event Functions” name to highlight that programming model: your function receives an event, processes it, and returns a response. The event-driven programming model is ideal for event processing, queue consumers, and invocations triggered by other AWS services. use an HTTP-native programming model with multi-concurrent execution and active CPU billing. Your code listens on a port and handles standard HTTP requests. That programming model is ideal for web applications, API backends, and interactive HTTP workloads where multiple concurrent requests and I/O-heavy processing are the norm. provide isolated, stateful sandbox environments with customer-managed lifecycles for running untrusted or user-generated code.The three complement each other. An application might use Event Functions for asynchronous backend processing, Web Functions for the user-facing API and server-side rendering layer, and MicroVMs for interactive code execution features, all within the same AWS account and sharing the same operational model.Modern web applications need to serve global audiences with fast, consistent response times while giving developers the freedom to use standard frameworks and iterate quickly. Building this today typically requires assembling multiple infrastructure components, configuring multi-region deployments, setting up latency-based routing, and tuning billing models to avoid paying for I/O wait. Developers often find themselves adapting their application code to fit a specific compute model rather than deploying the HTTP server they already have.Lambda Web Functions removes this complexity. Each execution environment handles multiple requests simultaneously (up to 128 concurrent requests per environment), which means a burst of incoming traffic can share warm environments instead of mapping each request to a new one. Web Functions meter the CPU time your code uses, so time spent waiting on a database query or an API call consumes little CPU. Memory is metered for as long as the execution environment exists. For I/O-heavy web workloads like server-side rendering, API backends, and AI inference endpoints streaming token responses, this can reduce compute costs.To get started, I wrote a simple Express.js server. This is the same code I run locally with , no Lambda-specific adapters or wrappers required.import express from 'express';

const app = express();
const [host, port] = process.env.AWS_LAMBDA_HTTP_ENDPOINT.split(':');

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  res.json({ message: 'Hello from Lambda Web Functions!' });
});

app.listen(port, host, () => {
  console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`);
});
The only difference from running locally is the  environment variable, which Lambda sets to the host and port your server must listen on. To run locally, set it yourself, for example AWS_LAMBDA_HTTP_ENDPOINT=127.0.0.1:3000 node index.js.aws lambda-web deploy --name my-web-app --code ./my-app
One command. No prior resource setup. The CLI bundled my source, created the Web Function, published its first revision, attached a Web Function endpoint, and returned a live HTTPS URL in under a minute.A revision is an immutable snapshot of the code I deployed. The endpoint is the HTTPS URL that routes traffic to a revision, or splits traffic across revisions. Those are the resources I use to iterate and to control how traffic moves to new code.$ curl https://<domain-id>.<shard-id>.lambda-web.us-east-1.on.aws/
{"message":"Hello from Lambda Web Functions!"}
Next, I wanted my application to serve users in Europe and Asia with lower latency. I redeployed with multiple regions:aws lambda-web deploy --name my-web-app --code ./my-app \
    --regions us-east-1,eu-west-1,ap-northeast-1
Lambda Web Functions replicated my code to all three regions and configured latency-based routing automatically. Each request now routes to the Region with the lowest latency for the caller, on a best-effort basis, through one global URL (<domain-id>.lambda-web.global.on.aws).When I updated my code and ran the same deploy command again, the CLI detected the existing function, uploaded the new revision, and pointed the endpoint at it. Total turnaround from code change to live: seconds.For production workflows that require a more gradual rollout, I can shift traffic between revisions with a single API call:aws lambda-web update-web-function-endpoint \
    --function-name my-web-app \
    --endpoint-name dev \
    --auto-deployment-mode Disabled \
    --revision-weights '[{"revisionId": "<current-revision-id>", "weight": 90}, {"revisionId": "<new-revision-id>", "weight": 10}]'
This sends 90% of endpoint traffic to the current revision and 10% to the new one, giving me a canary deployment without additional tooling.For applications that combine static and dynamic content, Web Functions works well alongside Amazon CloudFront. You can select a Web Function endpoint as the origin for your CloudFront distribution. Static assets are served from the cache while dynamic requests route to your Web Function, giving you CDN performance for static content with serverless compute for the dynamic parts.Web Functions improves the private-origin experience with CloudFront. With this launch, you attach CloudFront origin access control (OAC) to a Web Function endpoint instead. CloudFront signs requests to the origin, and the endpoint allows only your distribution to invoke it. You get the same private-origin pattern CloudFront already provides for Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3), without exposing the endpoint or adding custom authorization to your app.AWS Lambda Web Functions is available today in US East (N. Virginia), US East (Ohio), US West (Oregon), Asia Pacific (Tokyo), and Europe (Ireland). Web Functions run Node.js 24 (nodejs24.x) on Arm64 at launch and support Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) connectivity, allowing your function to access resources that exist within a VPC.To get started, deploy your first Web Function in under a minute:aws lambda-web deploy --name hello-world --hello-world
